From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] boringssl: recognise ppc64 as a 64-bit target

base.h dispatches on architecture to define OPENSSL_64_BIT or
OPENSSL_32_BIT, over a flat list: x86_64, x86, aarch64, arm, mips, mips64,
riscv64, riscv32, pnacl, wasm. powerpc is absent, so on ppc64le nothing
matches and *neither* word-size macro is defined.

crypto_word_t is then declared only under those macros:

    #if defined(OPENSSL_64_BIT)
    typedef uint64_t crypto_word_t;
    #elif defined(OPENSSL_32_BIT)
    typedef uint32_t crypto_word_t;

so it has no definition at all, and every translation unit that touches a
BIGNUM or a constant-time helper fails -- "unknown type name
'crypto_word_t'", "implicit declaration of bn_rshift_words",
"implicit declaration of constant_time_select_w", and so on through
crypto/, fipsmodule/ and the GCM modes.

Declare only the word size. Defining an OPENSSL_PPC64LE would opt into
per-architecture assembly that BoringSSL does not carry for us, so this
takes the generic C path, exactly as the asm-less targets above it do.
boring-sys2 already ships boringssl-loongarch.patch doing the same thing
for LoongArch.

--- a/deps/boringssl/src/include/openssl/base.h
+++ b/deps/boringssl/src/include/openssl/base.h
@@ -108,6 +108,9 @@
 #elif defined(__riscv) && __SIZEOF_POINTER__ == 4
 #define OPENSSL_32_BIT
+#elif defined(__powerpc64__) || defined(__PPC64__)
+// ppc64, both endiannesses.  Word size only -- see the patch header.
+#define OPENSSL_64_BIT
 #elif defined(__pnacl__)
